Subject: kms: do not read a closed response body when the KMS answers non-200

Carried downstream while arnika-project/arnika#44 is OPEN. Identical change to
that PR (its test file is not carried; this repository asserts the property
from its own build instead -- see the grep in the Dockerfiles that apply this).

Two defects in one loop, and the second is what makes the first reachable.

  1. The body was closed AFTER the retry decision while `res` kept its value,
     so a non-OK status on the final attempt left a CLOSED body in `res`.
     `io.ReadAll` below then fails with "http: read on closed response body".

  2. A 4xx/5xx is a successful HTTP transaction, so `err` is nil and the
     `if err != nil` check does not return. Nothing distinguished "the KMS
     refused" from a successful fetch.

Reproduced against Go 1.26.

WHY DOWNSTREAM AND NOT WAITING. This repository has done exactly this before:
the SetPSK peer-lookup fix was carried as a local patch until upstream merged
it as arnika#42, after which the patch was deleted and a `grep -q` assertion
kept in its place so a submodule bump backwards fails the build. The same
lifecycle applies here. Delete this file when #44 merges and the pin moves,
and leave the assertion behind.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM. It is NOT established that this fixes the
intermittent PPK mismatch in the `ipsec` CI job. That failure was diagnosed as
a BACKUP node running intervals behind, and whether a silently-failed KMS
fetch is upstream of it is unverified. This patch is carried because the
defect is real on its own terms; whether the failure rate moves is a
measurement to run, not an assumption to state.

diff --git a/repositories/kms.go b/repositories/kms.go
index ac74910..5b6a7b9 100644
--- a/repositories/kms.go
+++ b/repositories/kms.go
@@ -111,18 +111,31 @@ func (r *HTTPKMSRepository) kmsRequest(path string) (id string, key []byte, err
 		if err == nil && res.StatusCode == http.StatusOK {
 			break
 		}
+		// Close and clear BEFORE deciding whether to retry, so a body is never
+		// carried out of the loop after being closed. Previously the close
+		// happened after the retry decision and res kept its value, so a
+		// non-OK status on the final attempt left a CLOSED body in res, and
+		// io.ReadAll below failed with "http: read on closed response body".
+		if res != nil {
+			_ = res.Body.Close()
+			res = nil
+		}
 		if attempt < r.maxRetries {
 			delay := r.backoffBaseDelay * time.Duration(1<<uint(attempt))
 			log.Printf("Attempt %d: Retrying in %s...", attempt+1, delay)
 			time.Sleep(delay)
 		}
-		if res != nil {
-			_ = res.Body.Close()
-		}
 	}
 	if err != nil {
 		return "", nil, err
 	}
+	// A retry loop that never saw a 200 leaves res nil. Without this the caller
+	// cannot distinguish "the KMS refused" from a successful fetch, because a
+	// non-OK status is a successful HTTP transaction and sets no error.
+	if res == nil {
+		return "", nil, fmt.Errorf("KMS did not return %d after %d attempt(s)",
+			http.StatusOK, r.maxRetries+1)
+	}
 	defer func() { _ = res.Body.Close() }()
 
 	body, err := io.ReadAll(res.Body)
